tls (SChannel): TLS 1.3 where Windows speaks it

FFmpeg's SChannel asks Windows for credentials in the older form
(SCHANNEL_CRED), with which SChannel goes no further than TLS 1.2,
whatever the system allows. A server that speaks only TLS 1.3 could not
be reached: the handshake failed, and came out with the code for no
version agreed on (0002). Windows 11 and Windows Server 2022 (build
20348) speak TLS 1.3 as clients when given the newer form
(SCH_CREDENTIALS); Windows 10 does not, in either form.

So a client over TLS (not DTLS, and no server) asks for the newer form
there, with the flags it had and no restriction of its own on the
versions: the ones Windows' settings allow are offered, TLS 1.3 and 1.2
on Windows 11 as it comes. Everything else is as it was: SChannel still
judges no certificate, the client judges it itself (0003), and
revocation is still read from Windows' cache alone. Where Windows turns
the newer form down, and on every Windows 10, the client gets the older
form as before, so asking never costs a connection, and Windows 10 runs
the code it ran. The build is asked of ntdll (RtlGetVersion), whose
answer does not depend on the program's manifest. curl draws the line
at the same build.

TLS 1.3 also reaches a place in reading that TLS 1.2 seldom did. A TLS
1.3 server sends session tickets right after the handshake, OpenSSL two
at once, and SChannel's DecryptMessage returns SEC_I_RENEGOTIATE for
each: it is a handshake message after the handshake. FFmpeg's reader
then refused to go on whenever more had been received than that one
message ("Cannot renegotiate, encrypted data buffer not empty"), which
after the first of two tickets is always so, and the first read of
every TLS 1.3 connection failed. Microsoft's DecryptMessage (Schannel)
says to hand SChannel what is left, as the token of the next
InitializeSecurityContext, which tls_handshake_loop does with what is at
the head of the buffer; curl does the same. The refusal is gone.

Held to the TLS checklist of docs/Engine-Libs.md in the Windows 11
ARM64 virtual machine, ARM64 and x64; Windows 10 itself was not run
there.

